---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const P1 = `age ← 16
hasPass ← false

IF(age ≥ 18 OR hasPass)
{
  DISPLAY("Enter")
}
ELSE
{
  DISPLAY("Denied")
}`;
const P2 = `x ← 12
y ← 5

IF(NOT (x < y))
{
  DISPLAY("first")
}
IF(x > 10 AND y > 10)
{
  DISPLAY("second")
}
IF(x > 10 OR y > 10)
{
  DISPLAY("third")
}`;
const P3 = `n ← RANDOM(1, 4)
DISPLAY(n * 2)`;
const RefCard = `a ← expression          assign a copy of the result to a
DISPLAY(expression)      show the value, FOLLOWED BY A SPACE
INPUT()                  take a value from the user

+  -  *  /               17 / 5 is 3.4  (no truncation)
a MOD b                  remainder.  17 MOD 5 is 2
RANDOM(a, b)             whole number from a to b, BOTH INCLUDED

=  ≠  >  <  ≥  ≤            comparison.  = means EQUALS
NOT / AND / OR           boolean operators

IF(condition)            no ELSE IF exists - nest an IF inside an ELSE
{ ... }
ELSE
{ ... }`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Pseudocode Challenge: Combining Conditions</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>Three problems on <code>NOT</code>, <code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code>, and <code>RANDOM</code>. All three are traces, because reading someone else's boolean logic is exactly what the multiple-choice section asks you to do.</p>

          <p>
            <b>Do this on paper.</b> There is nothing to run and nothing to type.
            On exam day you get this notation on a printed sheet and no
            computer, so practicing it any other way practices the wrong thing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="reference">
          <h2>Reference Card</h2>

          <p>
            Everything you need for this page. This is the same notation the
            College Board uses on the exam.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={RefCard}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Exam Notation — what you have so far"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Two things catch people every time.</b>{" "}
            <code>DISPLAY</code> puts <b>a space</b> after whatever it shows, so
            two <code>DISPLAY</code> calls in a row give you
            <code>A B</code> on one line &mdash; not two lines and not
            <code>AB</code>. And <code>=</code> means <b>equals</b> here, not
            assignment. Assignment is <code>&larr;</code>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="problems">
          <h2>The Problems</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 1 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>What does this display?</p>

          <Code
            code={P1}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 1"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 2 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>What does this display?</p>

          <Code
            code={P2}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 2"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p><b>Careful:</b> these are three separate <code>IF</code> statements, not a chain. More than one can run.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 3 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>This program is run many times. List <b>every</b> value it could possibly display.</p>

          <Code
            code={P3}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 3"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p><b>Then answer this:</b> how many different values are possible? A student answers 3, reasoning that <code>RANDOM(1, 4)</code> gives 1, 2, or 3. Say what they got wrong.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Your three answers, with the working for Problem 3 shown as a list of the values that are possible.</p>

          <p>
            Worth <b>3 points</b>, graded on completion. Show your working for
            the traces &mdash; a wrong answer with visible reasoning is worth
            more to both of us than a right one with none.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
